        "id": 5395
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-11-21T14:26:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not super familiar with writing shaders (or optimizing them), so my only note is that there\u0027s a lot of branching here, which I vaguely recall to be Not Good for shader code if we can avoid it. Is there a way we could maybe write this to be branchless with step/mix/max/min and friends?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 61,
        "startChar": 9,
        "endLine": 61,
        "endChar": 11
      },
      "revId": "bf5180204e08d828aa49d442acac2332568563df",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bea0b814_61300d8a",
        "filename": "src/codec/SkHdrAgtm.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 61,
      "author": {
        "id": 6225
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-11-24T09:40:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The worst-case scenario for branching in a shader is that the shader core ends up executing all of the branches and then selecting the result. That is, historically, the very-very-very worst case scenario. And it\u0027s not that bad.\n\nBut, the last chip that I know of that did that was the NV40, which is ~20 years old. Nowadays (ever since G80, ~19 years ago, but I only know NV chips well), you only pay the price for divergence of branches within a \"warp\". A \"warp\" is a SIMD unit of 16 or 32 \"threads\", with each \"thread\" corresponding to a pixel. If all of the pixels take the same path, then you only pay for that path.\n\nSo I take a very cavalier attitude towards doing this sort of thing in shaders.\n\nWhat is, \"bad\", however, about this shader, is that I\u0027m reading the control points out of a texture. Ideally I would use a uniform buffer for that, but SKSL doesn\u0027t allow that sort of indexing. Reading from a texture is going to be fairly high latency compared to reading a uniform (probably). But, because this texture is microscopic, it\u0027s probably going to be sitting, in its entirety, in the closest cache.",
      "parentUuid": "e6552ab9_133822fd",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 61,
        "startChar": 9,
        "endLine": 61,
        "endChar": 11
      },
      "revId": "bf5180204e08d828aa49d442acac2332568563df",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
